event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1159,"item_id":"fc_0c7701ed84fdd119016adb0a5c09088191964ec5a5d478efc4","output_index":2,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"classification\\\":\\\"ROUTINE_LOCAL_REPAIR\\\",\\\"reviewed_location\\\":\\\"proof-draft.json, proof paragraph 2 (localization and matrix-unit argument), especially the rational-span/coordinate-ideal passage\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":\\\"The obstacle correctly identifies a missing justification in the draft, but that justification is available by a local repair that does not require descending M into the standard Q-form or changing the proof route. Treat M_R=R⊗_Z M as a full R-lattice in the original complex vector space, where R=Z_(p). The matrix units act on this complex vector space by their already-established formulas in the standard complex basis. Since their expressions use divided-power operators with coefficients in R, they preserve M_R. The eigenspaces need only be treated as complex lines: define J_i={c∈C : c e_i∈M_R}, not as subsets of Q. The matrix units give the required direct-sum decomposition and identify these R-modules. This closes precisely the descent issue without assuming a rational form containing M.\\\",\\\"same_route_assessment\\\":\\\"Yes. The corrected argument retains the draft's localization, divided-power matrix units, comparison of the arbitrary lattice's reduction with the standard module, and final matrix-unit irreducibility argument. It changes only the unjustified rational-coordinate claim to an argument on complex lines. It preserves the exact target, arbitrary admissible-lattice hypothesis, and all characteristics including 2 and λ=0.\\\",\\\"corrected_proof\\\":\\\"Fix p, K, λ and an admissible lattice M as in the target, and put R=Z_(p). Use the complex basis e_i=y^i v/i! (0≤i≤λ). The divided-power formulas are X_r e_i=binom(λ-i+r,r)e_(i-r) and Y_r e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r), with out-of-range terms zero, where X_r=x^r/r! and Y_r=y^r/r!. In particular these operators have integral matrices in this basis. Regard M_R=R⊗_Z M as a full R-lattice in V(λ), and let A_R be the image of U_Z⊗_Z R acting on V(λ). Admissibility implies A_R M_R⊆M_R.\\\\n\\\\nThe formulas give Y_λX_λ=E_λλ, and X_(λ-i)E_λλ=E_iλ. Also E_λλY_(λ-j)=binom(λ,j)E_λj. Since λ<p, binom(λ,j) is a unit in R for every j; hence all E_λj and E_ij=E_iλE_λj belong to A_R. These are identities of complex-linear operators on V(λ), and thus preserve M_R. (For λ=0 the identity operator is the sole matrix unit and the same conclusions below apply.)\\\\n\\\\nFor each i define the R-submodule J_i={c∈C : c e_i∈M_R}. It is finitely generated: M_R is finitely generated over R, and E_ii M_R=J_i e_i, so J_i e_i is the image of a finitely generated module under E_ii. It is nonzero: since M_R spans V(λ) over C, E_ii M_R spans the nonzero line C e_i over C. Applying E_ij to c e_j shows J_j⊆J_i, and applying E_ji gives the reverse inclusion. Thus all J_i equal one nonzero finitely generated R-submodule J of C. For each m∈M_R, the equality m=Σ_i E_ii m shows that each of its coordinate components lies in J e_i. Conversely, each J e_i is contained in M_R by definition. Therefore M_R=⊕_(i=0)^λ J e_i. As M_R is free of rank λ+1 over the discrete valuation ring R, this decomposition shows that J is a torsion-free R-module of rank one; equivalently it is a free rank-one R-module. In particular dim_(F_p)(J/pJ)=1. The decomposition identifies M_R/pM_R, with its operators x,y,h, with the standard reduced module having basis the images of e_0,…,e_λ: choose any R-module isomorphism R≅J and use it in each summand. Localization and reduction give M_R/pM_R≅M⊗_Z F_p, and extension of scalars gives the claimed identification after tensoring with K.\\\\n\\\\nFinally, on the standard reduced module, for every r≤λ<p, X_r and Y_r are scalar multiples of compositions of x and y, respectively, because r! is invertible modulo p. The matrix-unit identities above use only these divided powers and coefficients binom(λ,j), all nonzero in F_p. Thus the operators generated by x and y include every E_ij, also in characteristic 2. Any L(K)-submodule is stable under x,y and their compositions, hence under every E_ij. If it contains a nonzero vector, some coordinate c_i is nonzero; applying E_ji gives c_i e_j for each j, and scalar closure then gives every e_j. The submodule is the whole space. The module has dimension λ+1>0. This proves irreducibility for every stated M and K, including λ=0 (where it is one-dimensional).\\\",\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[]}\"}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":1160,"output_index":2,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0c7701ed84fdd119016adb0a5c09088191964ec5a5d478efc4","call_id":"call_e386df0fc0c3450999a4913cbe9d11c6","name":"submit_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"classification\\\":\\\"ROUTINE_LOCAL_REPAIR\\\",\\\"reviewed_location\\\":\\\"proof-draft.json, proof paragraph 2 (localization and matrix-unit argument), especially the rational-span/coordinate-ideal passage\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":\\\"The obstacle correctly identifies a missing justification in the draft, but that justification is available by a local repair that does not require descending M into the standard Q-form or changing the proof route. Treat M_R=R⊗_Z M as a full R-lattice in the original complex vector space, where R=Z_(p). The matrix units act on this complex vector space by their already-established formulas in the standard complex basis. Since their expressions use divided-power operators with coefficients in R, they preserve M_R. The eigenspaces need only be treated as complex lines: define J_i={c∈C : c e_i∈M_R}, not as subsets of Q. The matrix units give the required direct-sum decomposition and identify these R-modules. This closes precisely the descent issue without assuming a rational form containing M.\\\",\\\"same_route_assessment\\\":\\\"Yes. The corrected argument retains the draft's localization, divided-power matrix units, comparison of the arbitrary lattice's reduction with the standard module, and final matrix-unit irreducibility argument. It changes only the unjustified rational-coordinate claim to an argument on complex lines. It preserves the exact target, arbitrary admissible-lattice hypothesis, and all characteristics including 2 and λ=0.\\\",\\\"corrected_proof\\\":\\\"Fix p, K, λ and an admissible lattice M as in the target, and put R=Z_(p). Use the complex basis e_i=y^i v/i! (0≤i≤λ). The divided-power formulas are X_r e_i=binom(λ-i+r,r)e_(i-r) and Y_r e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r), with out-of-range terms zero, where X_r=x^r/r! and Y_r=y^r/r!. In particular these operators have integral matrices in this basis. Regard M_R=R⊗_Z M as a full R-lattice in V(λ), and let A_R be the image of U_Z⊗_Z R acting on V(λ). Admissibility implies A_R M_R⊆M_R.\\\\n\\\\nThe formulas give Y_λX_λ=E_λλ, and X_(λ-i)E_λλ=E_iλ. Also E_λλY_(λ-j)=binom(λ,j)E_λj. Since λ<p, binom(λ,j) is a unit in R for every j; hence all E_λj and E_ij=E_iλE_λj belong to A_R. These are identities of complex-linear operators on V(λ), and thus preserve M_R. (For λ=0 the identity operator is the sole matrix unit and the same conclusions below apply.)\\\\n\\\\nFor each i define the R-submodule J_i={c∈C : c e_i∈M_R}. It is finitely generated: M_R is finitely generated over R, and E_ii M_R=J_i e_i, so J_i e_i is the image of a finitely generated module under E_ii. It is nonzero: since M_R spans V(λ) over C, E_ii M_R spans the nonzero line C e_i over C. Applying E_ij to c e_j shows J_j⊆J_i, and applying E_ji gives the reverse inclusion. Thus all J_i equal one nonzero finitely generated R-submodule J of C. For each m∈M_R, the equality m=Σ_i E_ii m shows that each of its coordinate components lies in J e_i. Conversely, each J e_i is contained in M_R by definition. Therefore M_R=⊕_(i=0)^λ J e_i. As M_R is free of rank λ+1 over the discrete valuation ring R, this decomposition shows that J is a torsion-free R-module of rank one; equivalently it is a free rank-one R-module. In particular dim_(F_p)(J/pJ)=1. The decomposition identifies M_R/pM_R, with its operators x,y,h, with the standard reduced module having basis the images of e_0,…,e_λ: choose any R-module isomorphism R≅J and use it in each summand. Localization and reduction give M_R/pM_R≅M⊗_Z F_p, and extension of scalars gives the claimed identification after tensoring with K.\\\\n\\\\nFinally, on the standard reduced module, for every r≤λ<p, X_r and Y_r are scalar multiples of compositions of x and y, respectively, because r! is invertible modulo p. The matrix-unit identities above use only these divided powers and coefficients binom(λ,j), all nonzero in F_p. Thus the operators generated by x and y include every E_ij, also in characteristic 2. Any L(K)-submodule is stable under x,y and their compositions, hence under every E_ij. If it contains a nonzero vector, some coordinate c_i is nonzero; applying E_ji gives c_i e_j for each j, and scalar closure then gives every e_j. The submodule is the whole space. The module has dimension λ+1>0. This proves irreducibility for every stated M and K, including λ=0 (where it is one-dimensional).\\\",\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

